- Map each segment to one of the following content types: php, csharp, javascript, typescript, sql, rust, yaml, ruby, python, java, c, cpp, json, css, html, text, csv, shell, powershell, visual_basic, dockerfile, xml, markdown, go, svg, gettext-catalog, scala, swift, restructuredtext, tex, kotlin, dart, encoding_hex, encoding_base64, encoding_base32, encoding_base58, encoding_base85. If none of these apply (because a later rule requires a more specific label, or because the content is genuinely something else), use an open-set label of the form other_<best_guess> rather than plain "other". Open-set other_<best_guess> labels are normalized to other downstream for training, so prefer the more specific raw label whenever you can name the type.
- Be precise: e.g., classify JS/CSS embedded in HTML as javascript/css, HTML blocks inside Markdown as html, and so on.
- For React/JSX/TSX/Angular/Vue/Django and similar template DSLs, label tag wrappers and custom components with the appropriate open-set form (other_jsx, other_angular_template, other_vue_template, other_django_template, other_jinja_template, ...) and reserve javascript/typescript for the code inside {...} / {{ ... }} expressions. Plain HTML elements without DSL-specific syntax should stay html where possible (see the JSX rule below).
