- For Django or Jinja templates, label only the actual template syntax (e.g., {% ... %}, {{ ... }}) as other_django_template or other_jinja_template; the surrounding markup or text stays in its host language (typically html or markdown).
- Never label JSX tag wrappers (like <Square ... />) as javascript or html.
- In React/JSX/TSX, label as much as possible as html. A standard HTML tag span (for example, <code> with no attributes or only plain HTML attributes) stays html, even when it appears inside a surrounding JSX expression. Switch to other_jsx for any span containing JSX-specific syntax: className, JSX `{...}` expression delimiters, fragments (`<>...</>`), or custom-component tags such as <NewTag> or <MyButton> (PascalCase or otherwise non-standard element names). Expressions inside `{...}` are javascript/typescript. Heuristic: if a 0-shot reader would not interpret the span as plain HTML, it is probably not html; otherwise prefer html.
- Within HTML, the `<script>` and `<style>` tags themselves are html wrappers but their bodies are javascript and css, even when the tags live inside Markdown or strings; mirror this rule for any other language nest (e.g., SQL embedded in Rust strings).
- Important - split foreign-language injections in HTML-like regions. Inline style attributes must always be split: keep `style="` and the closing quote as html and place the style body in css, for every occurrence. Inline event-handler attributes (onClick, onmouseover, onkeydown, etc.) and `javascript:` URLs (e.g., `<iframe src="javascript:void(0)">`) must be split similarly: the surrounding attribute syntax stays as html, and the embedded code is typed as javascript. Drop markers inside the attribute without changing bytes. No CSS or JavaScript may remain inside an html segment.
- Template languages: in React/JSX/TSX, keep tag wrappers and custom components as other_jsx (never plain html). Angular templates that contain Angular syntax (e.g., *ngIf, [(ngModel)], {{...}}, (click)) use other_angular_template for the tag and text wrappers, while plain HTML without Angular syntax stays html. Vue templates (single-file components or inline templates) use other_vue_template for Vue-specific syntax such as v-if, v-for, :prop, @event, and {{ ... }}, while plain HTML without Vue syntax stays html. Django/Jinja templates use other_django_template or other_jinja_template for their blocks and tags. Inline `style` stays css; handlers and template expressions (e.g., onClick=..., {{ title }}, (click)="save()", {% if %}) are javascript/typescript or template-language content as appropriate.
- Comments stay with their host language (e.g., `#` in dockerfile / python). If a comment hides real executable code (such as JS inside a CSS comment), segment that code as its true language while keeping the surrounding comment bytes.
- Never invent continuation bytes or "helpful" closing tags if SOURCE_TEXT ends abruptly; stop exactly at the final byte even when braces or tags remain open.
- When you encounter encoded blobs (e.g., Base64 or hex) or other literal payloads, copy the bytes exactly once as-is and label them as encoding_base64 / encoding_hex / etc. rather than the surrounding language. Never decode, re-encode, summarize, interpret, or reproduce decoded content. Decide only from the visible characters and surrounding source context.
- Encoding labels apply to the literal payload characters even when the payload appears inside a quoted string, assignment, function argument, array, HTML element, heredoc, comment, or other host-language construct. Keep the surrounding quotes, operators, delimiters, and host syntax in the host language, but split the payload itself into the corresponding encoding class.
