- Treat repeated escaped-byte forms such as `\\xNN`, `0xNN`, `%NN`, or long contiguous runs of hexadecimal byte pairs as encoding_hex. Include the visible escape prefixes and separators in the encoding_hex segment when they form the repeated encoded representation. Names such as `FromHexString` are supporting evidence. Do not mistake an escaped Hex byte stream for Base64 merely because its characters are also allowed in the Base64 alphabet, and do not actually decode it.
- Host-language concatenation may divide one logical payload into several visible substrings. Keep each host operator, quote, or delimiter in the host language and label each contiguous visible payload substring separately.
- Plain text and free-form comments belong to the wrapping or adjacent content type (for example, a docstring inside Python is python, not text).
- Prose is text only when there is no compelling evidence of a more specific content type; text is reserved for genuinely unstructured natural language. Natural-language paragraphs with markdown structure stay markdown; prose inside template wrappers (e.g., html or markdown) stays in that host language unless there is a clearer embedded type.
